<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Tonal circle behind shuffle / previous / next / repeat in the full-screen player. Phone only;
     the TV layout keeps ripple_60dp.
     NEW FILE RATHER THAN CONTENT ADDED TO ripple_60dp, because that drawable is shared by seven
     sites including trackHeart and three TV controls, and it is deliberately CONTENTLESS - a bare
     <ripple> with no <item> draws nothing at rest. Adding an oval to it would put a circle behind the
     heart and behind the TV transport row as a side effect.
     48dp CIRCLE INSIDE A 64dp BOX, from the same 8dp inset the 72dp play/pause disc uses - so one
     inset value yields the two diameters and the 56dp-vs-48dp hierarchy is a consequence of the box
     sizes rather than a second number to keep in step. previous/next were wrap_content before this
     and are now pinned to 64dp for the same reason: a circle needs a known box. 64dp is below the
     row's 72dp play/pause container, so the ConstraintLayout's wrap_content height - and therefore
     playerControlsHeight and the collapse morph - is unchanged.
     ⚠️ THE OVAL IS OPAQUE WHITE ON PURPOSE. Both the resting and the ON colour arrive as a
     background TINT (android:backgroundTint here, PlayerFragment's playerColors observer at runtime),
     and AppCompat applies that as a SRC_IN colour filter: result alpha = tint alpha x shape alpha.
     A shape carrying its own 12% would multiply with the tint's 12% to ~1.4%. See the note at
     color/player_transport_circle_off.xml.
     ⚠️ MaterialButton LOGS A WARNING FOR android:background AND IT IS PRE-EXISTING, NOT NEW:
     previous/next/repeat already set ripple_60dp this way. The consequence that matters is that
     MaterialButtonHelper then does NOT wrap the background in its own InsetDrawable, so the 8dp
     inset above is the only inset in play and the circle really is 48dp. Decoded from the 1.14.0
     AAR: MaterialButton.setBackgroundTintList is unconditional (it calls setSupportBackgroundTintList
     directly, with no isUsingOriginalBackground branch), so tinting an overwritten background works
     through AppCompat for all three widget types here. -->
<ripple xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:color="@color/button_player_ripple"
    android:radius="24dp">
    <item>
        <inset android:inset="8dp">
            <shape android:shape="oval">
                <solid android:color="@android:color/white" />
            </shape>
        </inset>
    </item>
</ripple>
